# WP4-SIZING-amended: the four items, and one of them was a real defect

Supersedes `WP4-SIZING.patch`. Apply this one; it is the whole module and its tests, not a delta.

## 1. The maximum over heads was the wrong effect to size against

This is the item that was a defect rather than a discipline. `read_share_by_layer` reported only
the maximum, and `size_two_by_two` took it as the licensed share in the second branch. A quiet
head — total norm a thousandth of its neighbours' — with a span share of 0.9 would have licensed a
2 by 2 sized to detect an effect **nothing in the experiment can produce**, because the head
producing that 0.9 contributes almost nothing to the layer.

`ReadShare` already carried both norms, so the fix is to report both figures:

| figure | what it answers |
| --- | --- |
| `max_share`, the maximum over heads | **negligibility** — one head carrying the read is the case the rule exists to catch, and a mean would hide it |
| `layer_share`, magnitude-weighted | **how large an effect to size against** — `sqrt(Σ span²) / sqrt(Σ total²)`, the norm share of the concatenated per-head reads |

The test that separates them: one loud head at a share of 0.001 beside one quiet head at 0.9 gives
a maximum of 0.900 and a layer share of 0.00135.

`size_two_by_two` now takes a required `share_statistic` of `"max_head"` or `"layer"`, recorded in
the verdict and its inputs. The docstring states the intended use — negligibility on the maximum
at every layer, the licensed effect on the layer share — and says both are **pending the Head's
review**, since issue 82 puts admissibility with the Head. The function records the label rather
than enforcing a pairing, because which statistic licenses which branch is the Head's call and not
a fact about arithmetic.

## 2, 3, 4

**`matched_share_source` is required and recorded**, by the same argument that made
`matched_share_for` required: the verdict now says not only what the mapping produced but where it
came from. The tests' stand-in says "test stand-in" there.

**An all-undefined pre-check is refused**, not sized as negligible. That was my own zero rule
failing one level up: with every layer undefined the licensed share fell to 0.05 and the function
handed back a plannable n, turning "no read to attribute" into "the span contributes almost none
of the read" — the two findings the cell-level rule is careful to keep apart.

**`NOT_WORTH_RUNNING` fires on both branches**, and the comment now says so. A negligible
pre-check that cannot reach 0.05, and an observed share too small to detect, are the same answer
for different reasons; `every_layer_negligible` on the verdict tells them apart, and a test asserts
both fire and are distinguishable.

`ruff format` run over both files.

## Not done, and why

`read_share` stays pure-Python loops. It will be slow on real rows — 2,688 positions by 128 by 32
heads per layer — and vectorising it now would mean guessing the array type the adapter hands over.
When #81's adapter lands it accepts arrays and vectorises, same arithmetic.

## The lane constraint, as ruled

Issue 82's constraints say Codex implements and the Deputy commits. This is instrument arithmetic
with no model and no experiment, the Chief accepted it as mine, and the deviation is stated in the
issue comment with its reason rather than left implicit.

